@title "The Evolution of Destroyer Design"
@subtitle "Speed, Torpedoes, and Surface Warfare in the 1930s"

@h1 "Chapter 1"
@h2 "Origins and Tactical Doctrine"

Originally conceived as *torpedo boat destroyers* in the late nineteenth century, the destroyer rapidly evolved into the backbone of modern blue-water navies. By the 1930s, naval architects focused heavily on maximizing top speed and torpedo broadside weight while maintaining enough endurance for ocean-going fleet operations.

@figure {
    src: 'shimakaze_blueprint.png',
    label: fig:shimakaze
    width: 0.65,
    caption: "General arrangement profile of an experimental high-speed destroyer.",
    position: centered
}

## This pararaph is poorly redacted, improve ##

As illustrated in @ref(fig:shimakaze), hull lines were lengthened and narrowed significantly to achieve high length-to-beam ratios. This hydrodynamic optimization allowed destroyers to sustain speeds exceeding 38 knots in calm seas, outpacing capital ships and screening cruisers alike.

@h1 "Chapter 2"
@h2 "Propulsion and Armament Trade-offs"

Pushing a warship past 35 knots required immense shaft horsepower, forcing engineers to balance armor protection against machinery weight. Most interwar designs sacrificed hull plating entirely in favor of three core capabilities:

@list itemize
- *High-Pressure Boilers*: Superheated steam turbines capable of rapid acceleration during fleet engagements.
- *Heavy Torpedo Batteries*: Quadruple or quintuple launchers firing oxygen-enriched long-range torpedoes.
- *Dual-Purpose Artillery*: Enclosed turrets engineered to engage both surface targets and high-altitude aircraft.

## Pending for review!!! ##

@h2 "Operational Deployment Sequence"

During a standard nighttime torpedo run against an opposing battleline, destroyer squadrons executed a strict tactical sequence:

@list enumerate
- *Reconnaissance*: Floatplanes or forward pickets locate the enemy formation and relay bearing data.
- *Approach*: Squadrons advance at cruising speed under radio silence to minimize visual and radar detection.
- *Launch*: Ships execute a coordinated turn, firing a spread of torpedoes calculated for target speed and course.
- *Disengagement*: Vessels deploy chemical smoke screens and accelerate to flank speed to clear the engagement zone.

Ultimately, the versatility of these hulls ensured their survival long after the battleship era ended, laying the foundation for the guided-missile destroyers of the modern era.


